yki=β0+βq,1xq,ki+βq,12∑jk=1Jkxq,kj/Jk+βq,2∑kj=1J(xq,kixq,kj)/Jk+uki,1+uki,2+eki
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle y_{k_{i}}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{q, 1} x_{q, k_{i}}+\beta_{q, 12} \sum_{j_{k}=1}^{J_{k}} x_{q, k_{j}} / J_{k}+\beta_{q, 2} \sum_{k_{j}=1}^{J}\left(x_{q, k_{i}} x_{q, k_{j}}\right) / J_{k}+u_{k_{i}, 1}+u_{k_{i}, 2}+e_{k_{i}}$$\end{document}



, or


y=X0β0+X1β1+X12β12+X2β2+Zu1+Zu2+e
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{y}=\mathbf{X}_{0} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{0}+\mathbf{X}_{1} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{1}+\mathbf{X}_{12} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{12}+\mathbf{X}_{2} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{2}+\mathbf{Z} \mathbf{u}_{1}+\mathbf{Z} \mathbf{u}_{2}+\mathbf{e}$$\end{document}



, where

    (Eq.1)

(u1u2)∼MVN[(00),(σ12K1ρ12σ1σ2K12ρ12σ1σ2K21σ22K2)]
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \binom{\mathbf{u}_{1}}{\mathbf{u}_{2}} \sim \mathrm{MVN}\left[\binom{\mathbf{0}}{\mathbf{0}},\left(\begin{array}{cc}\sigma_{1}^{2} \mathbf{K}_{1} & \rho_{12} \sigma_{1} \sigma_{2} \mathbf{K}_{12} \\ \rho_{12} \sigma_{1} \sigma_{2} \mathbf{K}_{21} & \sigma_{2}^{2} \mathbf{K}_{2}\end{array}\right)\right] \quad$$\end{document}

In the case of Eq. 1, u1 and u2 are not independent due to their covariance. Therefore, the total phenotypic variance Var(y) can be decomposed as:
(Eq.2)
Var(y)=Var(u1+u2)+Var(e)=Var(u1)+Var(u2)+Cov(u1,u2)+Cov(u2,u1)+Var(e)=σ12K1+σ22K2+ρ12σ1σ2K12+ρ12σ2σ1K21+σe2I=σ12K1+σ22K2+ρ12σ1σ2(K12+K21)+σe2I(Eq. 2) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{y}) & =\operatorname{Var}\left(\mathbf{u}_{1}+\mathbf{u}_{2}\right)+\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{e}) \\ & =\operatorname{Var}\left(\mathbf{u}_{1}\right)+\operatorname{Var}\left(\mathbf{u}_{2}\right)+\operatorname{Cov}\left(\mathbf{u}_{1}, \mathbf{u}_{2}\right)+\operatorname{Cov}\left(\mathbf{u}_{2}, \mathbf{u}_{1}\right)+\operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{e}) \\ & =\sigma_{1}^{2} \mathbf{K}_{1}+\sigma_{2}^{2} \mathbf{K}_{2}+\rho_{12} \sigma_{1} \sigma_{2} \mathbf{K}_{12}+\rho_{12} \sigma_{2} \sigma_{1} \mathbf{K}_{21}+\sigma_{e}^{2} \mathbf{I} \\ & =\sigma_{1}^{2} \mathbf{K}_{1}+\sigma_{2}^{2} \mathbf{K}_{2}+\rho_{12} \sigma_{1} \sigma_{2}\left(\mathbf{K}_{12}+\mathbf{K}_{21}\right)+\sigma_{e}^{2} \mathbf{I} \text { (Eq. 2) } \tag{Eq.2} \end{align*}$$\end{document}

In addition to the fixed effects βq,1 and βq,2, uki\begin{document}$u_{k_{i}}$\end{document} denotes random effects that represent polygenic self and neighbor genotypic effects, respectively. Using vectors and matrices, Eq. S1 can also be expressed as a mixed model as follows:
(Eq.S1.2)
y=X0β0+X1β1+X2β2+Z1u+e \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{y}=\mathbf{X}_{0} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{0}+\mathbf{X}_{1} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{1}+\mathbf{X}_{2} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{2}+\mathbf{Z}_{1} \mathbf{u}+\mathbf{e}$$\end{document}

The original model Eq. S1.1 implicitly assumes symmetric effects between focal and neighboring individuals at the locus q by βq,2∑jk=1Jk(xq,kixq,kj)/Jk\begin{document}$\beta_{q, 2} \sum_{j_{k}=1}^{J_{k}}\left(x_{q, k_{i}} x_{q, k_{j}}\right) / J_{k}$\end{document}. This assumption can be relaxed by introducing an interaction term between DGE and IGE as follows:
(Eq.S2.1)
yki=β0+βq,1xq,ki+βq,12xq,ki∑jk=1J(xq,ikxq,jk)/Jk+βq,2∑jk=1Jk(xq,kixq,kj)/Jk+uki+eki \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} y_{k_{i}}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{q, 1} x_{q, k_{i}}+\beta_{q, 12} x_{q, k_{i}} \sum_{j_{k}=1}^{J}\left(x_{q, i_{k}} x_{q, j_{k}}\right) / J_{k}+\beta_{q, 2} \sum_{j_{k}=1}^{J_{k}}\left(x_{q, k_{i}} x_{q, k_{j}}\right) / J_{k}+u_{k_{i}}+e_{k_{i}} \tag{Eq.S2.1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

When genotypes values are encoded as {AA, Aa, aa} = {+1, 0, −1}, squared-genotype values xq,ki2\begin{document}$x_{q, k_{i}}^{2}$\end{document} turn +1 or 0. This leads to βq,12xq,ki∑kj=1Jk(xq,kixq,kj)/Jk=βq,12xq,ki2∑kj=1Jk(xq,kj)/Jk=βq,12∑kj=1Jk(xq,kj)/Jk\begin{document}$\beta_{q, 12} x_{q, k_{i}} \sum_{k_{j}=1}^{J_{k}}\left(x_{q, k_{i}} x_{q, k_{j}}\right) / J_{k}=\beta_{q, 12} x_{q, k_{i}}^{2} \sum_{k_{j}=1}^{J_{k}}\left(x_{q, k_{j}}\right) / J_{k}=\beta_{q, 12} \sum_{k_{j}=1}^{J_{k}}\left(x_{q, k_{j}}\right) / J_{k}$\end{document}, thereby making Eq. S2.1 simpler as follows:
(Eq.S2.2)
yki=β0+βq,1xq,ki+βq,12∑jk=1Jkxq,kj/Jk+βq,2∑kj=1J(xq,kixq,kj)/Jk+uki+eki \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} y_{k_{i}}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{q, 1} x_{q, k_{i}}+\beta_{q, 12} \sum_{j_{k}=1}^{J_{k}} x_{q, k_{j}} / J_{k}+\beta_{q, 2} \sum_{k_{j}=1}^{J}\left(x_{q, k_{i}} x_{q, k_{j}}\right) / J_{k}+u_{k_{i}}+e_{k_{i}} \tag{Eq.S2.2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

This Eq. S2.2 means that in the context of the Ising model, the statistical interaction term βq,12 can be exchanged as a main effect [56]. For clarity, we redefine βq,1 = βq,s; βq,12 = βq,n; and βq,2 = βq,s×n, which yields:
(Eq.S2.3)
yki=β0+βq,sxq,ki+βq,n∑kj=1Jxq,kj/Jk+βq,s×n∑kj=1J(xq,kixq,kj)/Jk+uki+eki \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} y_{k_{i}}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{q, s} x_{q, k_{i}}+\beta_{q, \mathrm{n}} \sum_{k_{j}=1}^{J} x_{q, k_{j}} / J_{k}+\beta_{q, \mathrm{~s} \times \mathrm{n}} \sum_{k_{j}=1}^{J}\left(x_{q, k_{i}} x_{q, k_{j}}\right) / J_{k}+u_{k_{i}}+e_{k_{i}} \tag{Eq.S2.3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

By focusing on allele frequency within a local space k, we can clarify how the three locus-wise coefficients βq,s, βq,n, and βq,s×n affect the focal phenotypic value yki\begin{document}$y_{k_{i}}$\end{document} [25]. Given that all the three datasets analyzed in this study included non-negligible fraction of heterozygous loci (see Methods), we assumed additive effects and encoded genotype values as {AA, Aa, aa} = {+1, 0, −1}. This holds the case 2 in Appendix S2 of Sato et al. [25]. According to Sato et al. [25], let us assume random mating and interactions among AA, Aa, and aa genotypes in the local space k where fq,A denotes the frequency of A alleles at q-th SNP locus. The phenotypic values for AA, Aa, and aa genotypes are then given as follows:
(Eq.S3)
yAA=β0+βq,s+(βq,n+βq,s×n)(2fq,A−1)yAa=β0yaa=β0−βq,s+(βq,n−βq,s×n)(2fq,A−1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} y_{\mathrm{AA}}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{q, \mathrm{~s}}+\left(\beta_{q, \mathrm{n}}+\beta_{q, \mathrm{~s} \times \mathrm{n}}\right)\left(2 f_{q, \mathrm{~A}}-1\right) \\ y_{\mathrm{Aa}}=\beta_{0} \\ y_{\mathrm{aa}}=\beta_{0}-\beta_{q, \mathrm{~s}}+\left(\beta_{q, \mathrm{n}}-\beta_{q, \mathrm{~s} \times \mathrm{n}}\right)\left(2 f_{q, \mathrm{~A}}-1\right) \tag{Eq.S3} \end{gather*}$$\end{document}

Other than our models, neighboring individuals can be specified using a design matrix or incidence matrix Z. Ordinal IGE models adopted this way to formulate polygenic DGEs and IGEs [5,11,57]. The ordinal IGE model can also be implemented using the RAINBOWR package as it allows users to define Z. Here, let the two design matrices be Z1 and Z2 for polygenic DGEs and IGEs, respectively. The ordinal model is then given as follows.
y=X0β0+Z1u1+Z2u2+e \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{y}=\mathbf{X}_{0} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{0}+\mathbf{Z}_{1} \mathbf{u}_{1}+\mathbf{Z}_{2} \mathbf{u}_{2}+\mathbf{e}$$\end{document}
where
(Eq.S4)
(u1u2)∼MVN[(00),(σ12ρ12σ1σ2ρ12σ1σ2σ22)⊗K1] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \binom{\mathbf{u}_{1}}{\mathbf{u}_{2}} \sim \mathrm{MVN}\left[\binom{\mathbf{0}}{\mathbf{0}},\left(\begin{array}{cc}\sigma_{1}^{2} & \rho_{12} \sigma_{1} \sigma_{2} \\ \rho_{12} \sigma_{1} \sigma_{2} & \sigma_{2}^{2}\end{array}\right) \otimes \mathbf{K}_{1}\right] \quad$$\end{document}
